denoising architectures commonly employed, such as Unet [10], CNNs [11], and Transformers [12], often fail to capture long-term dependencies adequately, making it difficult to maintain behavioral consistency over extended action sequences. This limitation may cause goal deviations during task execution, especially in dynamic, complex scenarios where a coherent policy is essential. Additionally, these architectures typically do not adequately incorporate short-term dependencies, resulting in reduced precision in the generated actions and fine-grained operations. The inability to balance long-term consistency with shortterm precision undermines the overall quality of action generation, highlighting the need for enhanced consistency and precision in diffusionbased GCIL frameworks to achieve high-quality long-horizon sequence modeling. To tackle these challenges, We prepose a novel diffusion-based generation framework, Mamba2Diff, with a core denoising network integrates the Mamba2 module [13], specifically to support long-horizon and multi-modal behavior generation in robotic manipulation. Leveraging Mamba2’s efficiency in multi-modal information processing and long-sequence modeling [14,15], Mamba2Diff enables stable behavior generation in high-dimensional, dynamic environments. Mamba2Diff introduces a bidirectional Mamba2 structure [16,17] that integrates forward and reverse information flows to capture comprehensive global contextual information, ensuring consistency and goal-oriented action sequences in long-horizon tasks. To further optimize the fusion of bidirectional information, we propose an Adaptive Allocation Gate (AAG) module to adaptively balance information flow based on task requirements. The combination of bidirectional Mamba2 and AAG constitutes the Bidirectional Gated Mamba2 (BDGM), which achieves more stable and consistent behavior generation over long horizons, enhancing the execution capabilities of robots in complex environments. Additionally, Mamba2Diff incorporates a Convolution-Enhanced Gated Recurrent Unit (CGRU) module, which combines convolutional processing with Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU)-based temporal modeling to capture both local spatial dynamics and sequential dependencies. This integration enables the model to track real-time changes in the environment and adapt to short-term variations, refining control over actions. By balancing long- and short-term dependencies, the design ensures consistency in long action sequences while enhancing accuracy and precision, particularly in complex, long-horizon tasks. As a result, Mamba2Diff enables accurate, coherent task executions, significantly improving robotic performance in dynamic environments. The primary contributions of this work are as follows:
1. Mamba2Diff is proposed to address the challenges of robotic manipulation in complex, dynamic environments, with a specific focus on long-horizon tasks-where it achieves high-precision action modeling via a multiscale, dependency-driven diffusion architecture, and enables real-time control through the integration of a dedicated improved denoising network architecture and a tailored efficient action generation process. 2. The proposed BDGM module leverages a bidirectional Mamba2 structure in conjunction with an AAG to effectively capture long-term dependencies, substantially improving the stability and consistency of task execution in challenging and dynamic settings. 3. The CGRU module integrates convolutional processing with GRUbased temporal modeling to capture short-term dependencies, boosting the precision of actions, particularly in tasks that require meticulous manipulation and fine-grained control. 4. Experimental results validate the superiority of Mamba2Diff, demonstrating enhanced consistency and precision compared to state-ofthe-art (SOTA) methods across three virtual long-horizon tasks and a custom-designed real-world environment.
