methods. Non-diffusion-based approaches typically employ generative models and sequence modeling architectures to enable agents to generate goal-oriented actions in multimodal environments. Notable examples of these architectures include Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) [23,24], Conditional Variational Autoencoders (CVAE) [24], and Transformers [25]. These models excel at capturing latent representations of multimodal behaviors that an agent may exhibit to achieve a given goal. In contrast, diffusion-based methods in GCIL leverage the iterative denoising process of Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) [26,27] and Score-Based Generative Models (SGMs) [10,19,28] to generate high-quality, goal-directed action sequences. This process begins with noisy action sequences, which are progressively refined toward the target goal through a series of denoising steps. By conditioning the diffusion process on both the current state and the desired goal, these methods are capable of generating precise long-horizon action sequences.
3.2. Diffusion models in robotic manipulation
Diffusion models were initially applied in image generation with DDPMs, as proposed in [29], demonstrating high-quality image generation through progressive denoising from noise. This technique was later adapted for robotic manipulation. However, its application does not involve directly generating control signals but instead supports robotic tasks by producing visual information. For example, ConstrainedContext Conditional Diffusion Models (C3DM) proposed by Saxena et al. [30] illustrate how task-relevant visual information can be generated to help robots disregard environmental distractions and focus on key areas of the task. Despite its benefits, this method’s limitation lies in the separation of perception and action during control, which can introduce delays and reduce control accuracy in highly complex environments. Beyond image generation, diffusion models have also been employed to generate reward functions for optimizing robot policies. For instance, the method proposed in [31] demonstrates how relative reward functions can be derived by comparing diffusion models with low and high rewards. However, a limitation of this approach is that the generated reward functions may generalize poorly to new tasks and environments, thereby restricting their applicability across varied scenarios. In addition to generating visual information and reward functions, diffusion models are increasingly used for direct action sequence generation in robotic manipulation. DDPM and SGMs have shown strong capabilities in generating high-dimensional action sequences by modeling multimodal distributions. [27] introduced the Diffusion Policy, which leverages DDPM to generate vision-based action policies for robots by gradually denoising action sequences to produce high-dimensional conditioned actions, enabling robots to perform complex tasks. However, the multi-step denoising process of this approach leads to lower inference efficiency, making it challenging to meet the real-time requirements of robotic manipulation. Additionally, Reuss et al. [19] proposed Behavior Generation with Score-Based Generative Policies (BESO), illustrating how score-based models can directly generate goal-oriented action sequences. Unlike reward-function-based methods, this approach models the multimodal distribution of robotic actions, allowing robots to execute goal-oriented tasks in complex environments. However, as task complexity and time horizons increase, this method reveals certain limitations in maintaining behavioral consistency and long-term planning, particularly in longhorizon tasks.
3.3. Denoising network structure design
Diffusion models utilize denoising networks to iteratively extract meaningful features, progressively transforming random noise into highquality outputs that align with target distributions. In robotic manipulation, the Unet architecture functions as a denoising network, generating trajectories through its encoder-decoder structure to support path planning in varied environments. The Render and Diffuse (R&D) proposed
in [32] leveraging a Unet-based denoising network to integrate robot actions and RGB observations in a unified image space, thus enhancing spatial generalization and sample efficiency in multimodal robotic tasks. Carvalho et al. [10] applied Unet in their motion planning diffusion model, successfully generating high-dimensional motion trajectories to address intricate path planning challenges. However, the local convolution operations of Unet struggle to capture global temporal dependencie in high-dimensional, long-sequence tasks, reducing the inference efficiency needed for real-time decision-making. CNNs have also been employed for precise action trajectory generation in complex environments due to their ability to capture local features. In the DiPPeR method proposed in [11], CNNs function as denoising networks within a diffusion model, significantly accelerating the inference process for path planning. Nonetheless, CNNs are limited by their computational complexity and localized feature extraction, which hampers denoising effectiveness in long-sequence tasks that require global contextual information. Transformers, in comparison, excel in high-dimensional signal denoising tasks by establishing long-term dependencies within input sequences through attention mechanisms. Hou et al. [12] employs a causal transformer as a denoising network, leveraging attention-based temporal dependencies to effectively align multimodal inputs and generate coherent, goal-oriented action sequences for complex robotic manipulation. Xian et al. [33] introduced the ChainedDiffuser method, which integrates a global transformer action predictor with a local trajectory diffuser, enabling the understanding of complex scene semantics while generating trajectory segments that bridge these macro actions. However, the computational complexity of Transformers increases substantially with larger input sizes, posing a significant obstacle to real-time robotic control. To address these challenges, we select Mamba2 as the denoising network for its efficiency in handling long-term dependencies and superior computational performance. Mamba2 combines the strengths of selective SSMs with deep learning, enabling dynamic adjustments in feature extraction to meet diverse task requirements while demonstrating enhanced robustness in complex environments and with multimodal inputs.
4. Methodology
As shown in Fig. 1(a), we propose Mamba2Diff, a novel method for robot motion generation in long-horizon tasks. It introduces the Multiscale Dependencies Fusion Block (MDFB) to enhance the denoising network’s ability to capture both long-term and short-term dependencies. By building on SGMs, Mamba2Diff generates smooth, high-quality motion sequences, improving both efficiency and precision, making it ideal for continuous control in complex tasks.
