4.3. Improved denoising network architecture
As illustrated in the Fig. 1(a), the denoising network employs a multimodule stacked architecture, with the MDFB as its core component. This module is designed to capture features across different temporal scales, enhancing the network’s ability to model both long-term and short-term dependencies. Additionally, the network incorporates the SwiGLU activation function to perform nonlinear feature mapping, further increasing its expressive capacity [39,40] (refer to Fig. 4 for the detailed structure of SwiGLU). Residual connections are applied following each MDFB and SwiGLU module to integrate the output back into the input, thereby improving model stability during training and effectively mitigating gradient vanishing issues. The complete denoising network is constructed by repeating these modules L times, leveraging multiscale dependency fusion and residual learning to enhance feature representation diversity and improve denoising performance for intricate sequence modeling.
