5. Simulation experiments
This section provides a comprehensive evaluation of Mamba2Diff through multi-dimensional experiments conducted in simulated environments. The evaluation not only validates Mamba2Diff’s effectiveness in capturing long-term dependencies and fusing multiscale dependencies, but also emphasizes its precision, consistency, and efficiency in long-horizon tasks. Specifically:
• Precision measures how accurately Mamba2Diff generates action sequences that align with the intended goals. • Consistency evaluates Mamba2Diff’s ability to maintain stable performance across extended task sequences, ensuring high-quality execution throughout. • Efficiency assesses the number of steps required for the robot to complete long-horizon tasks, with fewer steps indicating better optimization in action planning.
5.1. Experimental setup
Baselines: In goal-conditioned behavior modeling, Mamba2Diff is compared with current SOTA methods, categorized into diffusion-based and non-diffusion-based approaches. Non-diffusion-based methods include Goal-Conditioned Behavior Cloning (GCBC) [24], Latent Motor Plans (LMP) [42], Conditional Implicit Behavior Cloning (C-IBC) [43], Conditional-Behavior Transformer (C-BeT) [25], and Vector-Quantized Behavior Transformer (VQ-BeT) [44]. Diffusion-based methods, by contrast, primarily include CX-Diff [26], C-BESO [19], and CFG-BESO [19]. Experimental results for GCBC, LMP, C-IBC, C-BeT, CX-Diff, C-BESO, and CFG-BESO in the Block-Push and Relay Kitchen environments are reported from [19], while those for VQ-BeT in these environments are reported from [44]. Furthermore, following the protocol detailed in [44], we constructed the Multimodal Ant environment, successfully reproducing reward and result metrics for GCBC, LMP, C-IBC, C-BeT, CXDiff, C-BESO, and CFG-BESO. To ensure fairness in evaluation across all methods, we maintained consistency in general hyperparameters (e.g., layer size and depth) while adjusting method-specific hyperparameters as needed [19]. We evaluated Mamba2Diff against baseline methods using three simulation benchmarks, as illustrated in Fig. 5 and detailed in Table 1. Multimodal Block-Push [43]: This environment assesses the policy network’s ability to fit multimodal action distributions by requiring the robot to push two differently colored blocks into two matching colored squares in a random sequence. The environment consists of a 6-DoF
