5.6. Impact of denoising step setting
The configuration of denoising steps is critical in diffusion models as it directly influences task success rates and inference times. To determine the optimal setting, experiments were conducted in two simulation environments, Block-Push and Relay Kitchen, assessing how varying denoising steps l (with values: 1, 3, 5, 10, 20, 50) influence performance metrics. As illustrated in Fig. 11, the x-axis denotes the number of diffusion model iterations, while the y-axes show the result metric (left) and the average inference time per full task sequence (right). In the Block-Push environment, increasing l initially enhances the result metric, peaking at an average value of 0.97 at l = 3, after which performance slightly declines and stabilizes at higher values. For l ≥ 3, the result metric remains relatively steady. In contrast, inference time consistently increases with higher l, from an average of 3.76 seconds at l = 1 to 8.63 seconds at l = 50. A similar trend was observed in the Relay Kitchen environment, where the result metric reached its highest aver
age value of 3.84 at l = 3 before a gradual stabilization at higher steps. In this environment, inference time increased more notably, ranging from an average of 3.65 seconds at l = 1 to 19.55 seconds at l = 50, highlighting the higher computational cost associated with larger denoising steps. Overall, these results suggest that a denoising step of l = 3 achieves the best balance for the Mamba2Diff algorithm in both environments, providing an optimal trade-off between task success rate and inference time.
5.7. Computational cost and real-time efficiency analysis
Unlike traditional diffusion models such as CX-Diff and C-BESO, which rely on MLP or Transformer-based architectures for their denoising networks, Mamba2Diff adopts a novel bidirectional Mamba2 structure, offering significant advantages in computational cost and realtime efficiency. The bidirectional flow of information in our proposed MDFB modules enables more efficient long-range dependency modeling and parameter utilization, thereby accelerating the convergence of the denoising process. As shown in Table 4, compared to diffusion models (CX-Diff, C-BESO), Mamba2Diff reduces the number of parameters by 28 %-45 % (e.g., in the Multimodal Ant environment, CX-Diff has 1.4 times more parameters), while achieving 1.9-8.1 times faster inference speeds (e.g., in the Multimodal Ant environment, CX-Diff takes 8 times longer), demonstrating its dual advantages in both computational cost and real-time performance. Compared to non-diffusion lightweight models (VQ-BeT), Mamba2Diff achieves comparable or even lower parameter counts, while maintaining or even surpassing inference speed (e.g., in the Relay Kitchen environment). Additionally, Mamba2Diff retains the unique ability of diffusion models to generate fine-grained action sequences, making it more competitive in balancing real-time performance and task precision. All experiments were conducted on a hardware system equipped with an Intel Core i5-14600KF CPU, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 D Graphics Processing Unit (GPU), and 32 GB of RAM.
