6. Real-World experiments
In the previous sections, we validated the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm through experiments conducted in three distinct simulation environments. Building on these results, we constructed a realworld robotic platform (as shown in Fig. 16) and collected human demonstrations to assess the algorithm’s performance in a practical setting.
Environment Setup and Data Collection: The primary robotic agent used in our real-world setup is a UR5E arm with a DaHuang AG95 gripper attachment. Observational inputs for training the policy network come from two sources: a D435 RGB-D wrist camera mounted on the robotic arm, which captures precise information about the gripper’s immediate workspace, and a D455 environment camera used to capture global scene information. Following the experimental protocol from [27], we used a Gello [50] to operate the robot remotely at a 30Hz frequency, collecting six degrees of freedom (6-DoF) human demonstrations. In total, we collected 576 trajectories across six different operators. As outlined in [51], we performed random geometric sampling immediately after each task’s completion point to obtain goal images. Scenarios Design: To evaluate the proposed algorithm’s capability in long-horizon tasks, we designed two real-world scenarios (as shown in Fig. 17). In the first scenario, a can is scattered on the table, alongside a basket containing several cans and a shelf intended for final storage. The task requires the robot to first place the scattered can into the basket, lift the basket, and finally place it on the shelf. This scenario includes five phases, with the robot needing to execute four steps to complete the task. The second scenario features a bag of items and a hinge cabinet,
