Model Training and Evaluation: We reproduced the CX-Diff, CBESO, and VQ-BeT algorithms on our real-world platform and used them as benchmarks to validate the advantages of our proposed Mamba2Diff in executing long-horizon tasks. Among the four methods, CX-Diff required the longest training time at approximately 4 hours, while the other algorithms each took about 2 h on an Nvidia RTX 4090 D GPU. During evaluation, each real-world scenario was repeated 20 times to mitigate random variation. To comprehensively assess the policy network’s overall task completion capability across both scenarios and its sustained performance in long-horizon tasks, we introduced two evaluation metrics: Completion Count (CC) and Overall Success Rate (OSR). Result Analysis: In real-world experiments, we compared Mamba2Diff with three baseline algorithms (CX-Diff, C-BESO, and VQ-BeT), recording each task’s average completion count (CC) and overall success rate (OSR). The experimental results for two realworld scenarios are presented in Table 6 and Table 7. Mamba2Diff demonstrated superior performance, particularly in the later, more challenging stages of long-sequence tasks, achieving average OSRs of 94.75 % and 91.25 % in the first and second scenarios, respectively, significantly outperforming the baseline methods. Specially, in task 4 of the first scenario-a critical later-stage task-Mamba2Diff achieved an average CC of 18 out of 20 attempts, compared to 15, 14, and 11 for VQ-BeT, C-BESO, and CX-Diff, respectively. Similarly, in task 4 of the second scenario, Mamba2Diff averaged 17 completions out of 20 attempts, again surpassing the performance of the baseline algorithms. These findings underscore Mamba2Diff’s enhanced stability and execution capability in later, more complex stages of long-sequence tasks, demonstrating its adaptability for multi-step planning and complex task execution.
