7. Conclusion
In this study, we propose Mamba2Diff, a novel diffusion framework that fuses multiscale dependencies for long-horizon robotic manipulation. By integrating the BDGM and CGRU modules, Mamba2Diff captures both long-term and short-term temporal features, enabling precise and consistent action generation in complex environments. The framework is evaluated across three simulated environmentsBlock-Push, Multimodal Ant, and Relay Kitchen-as well as a custom real-world platform. Results show that Mamba2Diff achieves superior performance in terms of precision, efficiency, and temporal consistency. Specifically, success rate improvements over state-of-the-art methods are 2.7 %, 1.5 %, 2.2 %, and 8 % in the Block-Push, Ant, Kitchen, and real-world settings, respectively. In long-horizon tasks, Mamba2Diff
consistently outperforms diffusion-based and non-diffusion-based baselines across all stages of execution. Notably, in the final phases of task completion, it achieves success rate gains of 5 %, 4 %, and 12.5 % in the Kitchen, Ant, and real-world environments, respectively. These findings highlight the effectiveness of Mamba2Diff’s multiscale modeling strategy and its potential for robust control in highdimensional, dynamic tasks. The framework offers a solid foundation for real-world deployment in domains such as autonomous logistics and domestic assistance. Future work will explore its extension to broader robotic tasks.
